# Service layers

## Contracted (`app/` + `app/impl/`)

The interface is the contract. Controllers inject that interface or a port — never
`@InternalService` or `app/internal` types. Name after the use case
(`ProfileImageService` / `ProfileImageServiceImpl`).

**JDBC-only:** put `@Transactional` (or `readOnly`) on the contracted impl and call
repositories there. Multiple repository calls in one method are one transaction.
Do not add an `@InternalService` for JDBC-only work.

## Internal (`app/internal/`)

**`@InternalService`:** use **only** when the use case mixes outbound I/O (S3, HTTP,
queues) with JDBC. I/O stays on the contracted impl; JDBC stays on this bean so
the transaction does not wrap the I/O. Name after the record it owns
(`ClassroomRecordService`), not the caller — the name must still make sense with
every other class deleted. One internal type may serve more than one contracted
service.

**Utility classes:** `app/internal/` may also hold small helpers (`*Utils`) that
support contracted services. No Spring bean, no JDBC of their own. Do not inject
them into controllers.
